<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Mesoamerica and the Caribbean</p><h1 class="st-title">Antigua and Barbuda</h1><p class="st-meta">1981 CE – present · capital St John's</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Two-island Leeward state independent since 1981, long dominated by the Bird family and now reliant on tourism.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/colony-of-antigua">Colony of Antigua</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barbuda">Barbuda</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/redonda">Redonda</a>, became independent on 1 November 1981. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vere-bird">Vere Bird</a>, the trade unionist who had led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antigua-labour-party">Antigua Labour Party</a> since the 1940s, became the first prime minister, with the British monarch as head of state. Barbudans had sought separate status and remained uneasy partners.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antigua-labour-party">Antigua Labour Party</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vere-bird">Vere Bird</a> and then his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lester-bird">Lester Bird</a> governed until 2004, amid scandals including the shipment of Israeli arms through <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/colony-of-antigua">Antigua</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medellin-cartel">Medellín cartel</a> in 1989 to 1990. Tourism, with beaches and the restored <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nelsons-dockyard">Nelson's Dockyard</a>, replaced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a>, and the country also developed offshore finance and online gambling. The collapse of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allen-stanford">Allen Stanford's</a> banking empire in 2009 was a severe blow.</p><h2>To the present</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baldwin-spencer">Baldwin Spencer's</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-progressive-party-antigua-and-barbuda">United Progressive Party</a> ruled from 2004 to 2014, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaston-browne">Gaston Browne</a> has led governments since 2014. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hurricane-irma">Hurricane Irma</a> forced the evacuation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barbuda">Barbuda</a> in 2017, and disputes over communal land on Barbuda have continued.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/antigua-and-barbuda.jpg" alt="Antigua and Barbuda" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Original: Sir Reginald Samuel Vector: David Benbennick · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Antigua_and_Barbuda.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1981 CE – present</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>St John's</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Vere Bird, Lester Bird, Gaston Browne</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Anglicanism and Protestantism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>English and Antiguan Creole</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/antigua-and-barbuda">Antigua and Barbuda</a></dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=2004&amp;focus=antigua-and-barbuda" aria-label="Antigua and Barbuda on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2998 1542 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Antigua and Barbuda lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3318 1798h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3318 1798h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=2004&amp;focus=antigua-and-barbuda">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
